@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Polling";
}

@* data-signals__ifmissing only fills in signals that don't exist yet, so a
   re-render (or a patch that arrives first) never clobbers the live values. *@
<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl"
     data-signals__ifmissing="{polling: true, ticks: 0, patches: '', stats: {requests: 0, load: 0}}">

    <h1 class="page-title">Polling &amp; morph control</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">
        The <a class="link" asp-action="LiveStream">Live Stream</a> page holds one SSE connection
        open. This is the other option: short requests on a timer, driven entirely by
        <code class="code-inline">data-on-interval</code>. Because the same element is re-patched
        every two seconds, it's also the right place to show what morphing keeps and what it
        overwrites.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">

        @* ── 1. The poll loop ──────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        @*
            data-init fires the first request as soon as the element is set up, and
            data-on-interval repeats it. The interval always ticks; guarding the
            expression on $polling is how you pause it.
        *@
        <div class="card space-y-4"
             data-init="@@get('/api/stats')"
             data-on-interval__duration.2s="$polling && @@get('/api/stats')">

            <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
                <h2 class="card-title">1. Poll every 2 seconds</h2>
                <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="h-2.5 w-2.5 rounded-full"
                          data-class="{'bg-green-500': $polling, 'bg-gray-400': !$polling}"></span>
                    <button class="btn-secondary"
                            data-on:click="$polling = !$polling"
                            data-text="$polling ? 'Pause' : 'Resume'"></button>
                </div>
            </div>

            @{ var initialStats = new Essessay.Models.StatsViewModel(0, 0, DateTimeOffset.Now); }
            <partial name="_Stats" model="initialStats"/>
        </div>

        @* ── 2. Reacting to signal patches ─────────────────────────────────── *@
        @*
            Each poll sends two SSE events: an element patch and a signal patch.
            data-on-signal-patch fires on the second one. The -filter attribute limits
            it to stats.* — without a filter, the $ticks and $patches writes below would
            re-trigger the handler.
        *@
        <div class="card space-y-3"
             data-on-signal-patch="$ticks++; $patches = $patches + `${new Date().toLocaleTimeString()}  ${JSON.stringify(patch)}\n`"
             data-on-signal-patch-filter="{include: /^stats\./}">

            <h2 class="card-title">2. Reacting to server-patched signals</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                <code class="code-inline">data-on-signal-patch</code> receives the filtered
                <code class="code-inline">patch</code> object. Below, two side effects:
                <code class="code-inline">data-effect</code> re-runs whenever a signal it reads
                changes — one keeps the browser tab title in sync, the other scrolls the log,
                using a DOM node captured by <code class="code-inline">data-ref</code>.
            </p>

            <p class="text-sm">
                Patches seen: <span class="font-mono font-semibold" data-text="$ticks"></span>
            </p>

            @* Reading $polling and $ticks is what subscribes this effect to them. *@
            <div data-effect="document.title = ($polling ? '● ' : '❚❚ ') + `Polling (${$ticks}) - Essessay`"></div>

            @*
                The signal name goes in the value, not the key: HTML lowercases
                attribute names, so `data-ref:logEl` would create `$logel`.
                $patches is read to register the dependency, and the scroll is
                deferred so it runs after data-text has replaced the content.
            *@
            <pre class="log-pane"
                 data-ref="logEl"
                 data-text="$patches || 'Waiting for the first patch…'"
                 data-effect="$patches; $logEl && requestAnimationFrame(() => $logEl.scrollTop = $logEl.scrollHeight)"></pre>
        </div>

        @* ── 3. Morph control ──────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <div class="card space-y-3">
            <h2 class="card-title">3. Keeping client state across a patch</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                Morphing makes the DOM match the server's markup, which means client-side state
                inside a patched region is normally lost. Open the two
                <code class="code-inline">&lt;details&gt;</code> boxes in card 1 and watch which one
                stays open: <code class="code-inline">data-preserve-attr="open"</code> tells the morph
                to leave that attribute alone, and <code class="code-inline">data-ignore-morph</code>
                takes a whole subtree out of the morph.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                <code class="code-inline">data-ignore</code> is different again — it stops Datastar
                from processing an element and its descendants at all. Both buttons below carry the
                same <code class="code-inline">data-on:click</code>, but only the first one was wired up:
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3" data-signals="{clicks: 0}">
                <button class="btn-secondary" data-on:click="$clicks++">Counts</button>
                <span data-ignore>
                    <button class="btn-secondary" data-on:click="$clicks++">Inert (inside data-ignore)</button>
                </span>
                <span class="text-sm">clicks: <span class="font-mono font-semibold" data-text="$clicks"></span></span>
            </div>
        </div>

        @* ── 4. Signal inspector ───────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <div class="card space-y-3">
            <h2 class="card-title">4. Signal inspector</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                <code class="code-inline">data-json-signals</code> dumps the live signal tree into an
                element — the fastest way to see what the server actually patched. It takes the same
                include/exclude filter as <code class="code-inline">filterSignals</code>, which is
                how the page-wide <code class="code-inline">$csrf</code> token and the long
                <code class="code-inline">$patches</code> log string are kept out of the way here.
            </p>
            <pre class="log-pane" data-json-signals="{exclude: /csrf|patches/}"></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
